\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The exact tangency, in the scaling limit $m\to \infty $ (everything per unit of level, $\rho =s/m$). The blade cone $6\rho ^2$ (pink) touches the shore line $(12+4\sqrt  3)\rho -(8+4\sqrt  3)$ (cyan) at exactly one point, $\rho ^*=1+1/\sqrt  3$: the discriminant vanishes identically. Windows exist only in the shaded zone $\rho \le \sqrt  {5/3}$, strictly left of the tangency --- which is precisely why the theorem holds.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:tangent}{{1}{3}{The exact tangency, in the scaling limit $m\to \infty $ (everything per unit of level, $\rho =s/m$). The blade cone $6\rho ^2$ (pink) touches the shore line $(12+4\sqrt 3)\rho -(8+4\sqrt 3)$ (cyan) at exactly one point, $\rho ^*=1+1/\sqrt 3$: the discriminant vanishes identically. Windows exist only in the shaded zone $\rho \le \sqrt {5/3}$, strictly left of the tangency --- which is precisely why the theorem holds}{figure.1}{}}
